  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 209 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nToday and tomorrow will bring beneficial rainfall to a majority \nof the area with an isolated risk of flash flooding. High \ntemperatures will reach the low to mid 90s once again today across\nthe plains. There are already several storms ongoing across the \nhigh country and the Palmer Divide. Storms today will migrate into\nthe plains by mid-afternoon and push east into the early evening.\nScattered showers and thunderstorms will likely continue across \nour area through at least midnight tonight.\n\nLow-level flow is currently southeast which is continuing to \nprovide ample moisture to our area. Additionally, we already have \nMLCAPE of 500-1000 J/kg and 20-30 knots of bulk shear as of early \nthis afternoon. Storms will be outflow dominant, so any storm \ncould produce gusty winds up to 50-60 mph with the highest risk \nacross the plains.\n\nPWATs are at 150-200% of normal today and will approach \nclimatological record high values on Thursday. We have a low \nflooding risk today given slow storm motions and the warm cloud \nlayer depth approaching 5000 feet. The highest risk of flooding \nwill be across the high country, mainly for burn areas and also \nwhere storms train or for areas that received significant rainfall\nyesterday (and thus have lower flash flood guidance). \n\nA shortwave moves through tomorrow during the day which should \nprovide a slight increase in rainfall coverage tomorrow afternoon \nas well as high temperatures in the upper 80s for the plains. \nStorms will once again form in the high country by late morning to\nearly afternoon and then move into the plains during the \nafternoon and evening. Storm motion will be faster than today \n(around 20 kts) given increased flow aloft, but tomorrow still \ncarries a low risk for flash flooding. Additionally, the warm \ncloud layer should be deeper than today allowing for more \nefficient warm rain processes. Tomorrow will likely be the 3rd day\nin a row of rainfall for some areas. Rainfall rates could once \nagain approach 2-3 inches per hour, so the WPC is now highlighting\nmost of our area in a slight (2 out of 4) risk for flooding. \n\nIn summary, there are several ingredients in place that lead us \nto be concerned about the potential for isolated instances of \nflash flooding in our area, but also some ingredients that will \nlessen the extent of the threat. Given the nature of these storms,\nthere will be varying rainfall totals across short distances. \nOverall, additional rainfall totals across the area will generally\nbe around 0.25 to 0.75 inches with some isolated totals of 2-3+ \ninches by Thursday evening. \n\nThe high pressure will build back into the area starting Friday \nand strengthen through the weekend. The last several model runs \nhave been keying in on the heat peaking on Sunday. This is when \ntemperatures across the plains could reach 100 degrees F once \nagain, given the thermal ridge aloft will be near 20 C and \ncentered over our area. Record highs could be in play and a Heat \nAdvisory could be needed as we get closer to the weekend. A chance\nfor scattered showers is expected to continue through the weekend\nfor the high country. \n\nBy Monday and Tuesday, models show the ridge weakening and \nshifting slightly off to the southeast. At the same time, moisture\nfrom the remnants of Bertha could be pulled into the Southwest \nUnited States and into western Colorado. This will be something to\nwatch as this could bring an increase in rain chances, mainly for\nthe high country. Otherwise, highs should still be in the mid to \nupper 90s for the plains through much of next week.",
